 clearly  that this is a specific marker for layer  6b in the in the mouse cortex but the  big difference here is that the dataset  shown on the low part so so for each  gene we just colored this in green right  but basically  each of these panels are comprising of a  huge data set where we actually  multiplexed up to 100 g so  that data set is much richer and we we  just pick out the the the gene we want  to just visualize that and and and do  further analysis so that adds more um  depth to the data set we have compared  to of course  low multiplexed in situ hybridization  and as for sensitivity so when we  compare that with bulk rna-seq coming  from the human protein atlas rna-seq  database for mouse brain  we can nicely see that there's a good  correlation with the counts we we we  have from the molecular cartography so  genes that are expressed highly in in  the rna-seq data  set will also be highly expressed in our  data set and vice versa for low express  g  but i think more importantly  we want to of course compare that to  another spatial technology and in this  case single molecule fish because it's  considered to be the gold standard and  the most sensitive  spatial technology available to date  so we compared our technology with  single molecule fist just for a couple  of genes  and we we see that the counts per cell  are almost identical so we have similar  sensitivity as compared to single  molecule fish  so let's dive deeper into what we can  learn from  data sets  in neuroscience so again  this is the the mouse brain hemisphere  and within the set of 100 genes that we  selected we just visualize here 13 of  those genes and these 13 genes are  actually markers for the different  cortical layers  and zoomed in centrally in the slide you  can clearly see that  as we expected all these different  markers it's a beautiful rainbow picture  here  but it nicely shows that we are able to  constantly  recapitulate what is known to be  specific for the the different cell  layers and that these that these marker  genes are are nicely located to where we  expect them to be so that's a nice  validation of the the marker genes we  put in and and the validation of our  data set as well  but you can go further so what is nice  is within the same data sets you can  actually  really dive deeper into all the  different cell types so you can clearly  see here that in the different shades of  red we visualize the glutamatergic  neurons  but also in yellow the gabaergic  interneurons  and also microglia astrocytes  oligodendrocytes  so these are all nicely visible in this  data set of course it is a lot the data  is highly dense but it also shows nicely  how many individual data points you're  able to capture just from 35 genes so  not not even the whole data set but you  can still clearly  see already for example in yellow these  gabaergic interneurons nicely  interspersed with with the glutamatergic  neurons beside it  um but of course i think it's it's nice  to dive even deeper into the data and  really  a lot it allows you to just select a  subset of genes you're interested in and  this is a great example of it so if we  only look at certain subtypes of  inhibitory neurons when we just look at  the marker genes for that  for example we have get one or pair of  albumin positive interneurons  somatostatin in yellow and vip  interneurons in in purple you can nicely  appreciate i think the the the  complexity and the way they are  organized and even  especially for the somatostatin  interneurons here you can even see the  projections coming out of the the cell  body here which is a nice um thing to  see if you're interested in neuroscience  to also see all those details popping up  and then importantly here i want to show  you a  visual representation  of one single somatostatin interneuron  and this is really to show that we are  for one uh able to um  to capture 3d information uh in in in  our data sets um and this is nicely  shown by the doppy segmented nuclei  and you see each individual spot  represents one individual transcript  molecule coming from somatostatin you  can nicely appreciate that  even  zooming in into one individual cell like  this it allows you to really  dive deeper into subcellular differences  either within a given cell or comparing  that to other cell types as well so our  resolution is really subcellular uh down  to the single molecule level  so  more downstream analysis of course can  be done and one way to do that is of  course taking that whole data set  and segment out each individual cell of  course segmentation is a topic in itself  it's a very complex topic especially in  neuroscience because all these cells  have very complex shapes  but that's a topic i think for another  webinar  today i want to show you if we just take  a basic cell segmentation based on dapi  we're already  seeing a lot of information popping up  we can generate cell to gene and gene to  cell matrices and what you can see is  per individual segmented cell here we  are able to pick up  hundreds to sometimes thousands of  individual signals per cell and also uh  upwards to 60 sometimes more genes per  cell that are actually expressed and  this is in a panel of 100g so that tells  you already a lot of the  the intricate details of the data set  and and how much information you  actually capture  but using that  using that cell segmentation  what it allows you to do is  really  just like in single cell rna-seq  it allows you to  take that information take that matrix  and do  again uh clustering on that so it's  completely compatible with for example  syrup and in this example on the left  panel you can see  for 35 000 individual cells based on the  100 genes  data set i showed you earlier and you  can see that all these clusters  are very nicely defined and we were able  to  identify all the major  cell types in this and on the right side  you can see the heat map and this rule  will allow you to identify  different marker genes  that are  specific for each individual cluster  but of course  very different from single cell rna  sequence we have here quite  familiar looking data i think but what  is important here is that we always  retain the spatial dimension here and  the spatial structure so what is shown  here is you can always go back and for  example select a certain cluster that  you're interested in interested in  and see how it actually maps  spatially in in this section uh where  you captured the data originally from so  here is an animation that actually shows  all these different clusters being  projected back onto the tissue and this  really allows you to understand much  better how all these individual cells  and these these different cell clusters  are are  spatially mapped and this really  provides  the spatial context whenever it's needed  you can always go back to that again  and this is also represented here in  this slide where  we are nicely able to  visually represent all the different  cell types on on the tissue section  whether it be oligodendrocytes or again  the glutamatergic neurons or micro  microglia you can really appreciate all  the different  spatial organizations of these cell  types  and what you can do because we have the  segmented uh  self-segmented data from that you can  actually just use that clustering and  count all those different cells and see  for example in a simple analysis how  many um glutamatergic neurons are there  compared to other cell types and this we  did this on our data and it really  nicely recapitulates what is known  in in the mouse brain already so it's  again a nice validation of of our  technology and what you can do with it  and and this really opens up the whole  new spatial dimension in in your single  cell data set  but i want to talk also today more about  certain applications  what you can do so for example what we  did here i want to explain a bit more  about  what we did in a collaboration with a  major pharmaceutical company and this  was in the context of alzheimer's  disease so  in alzheimer's disease mouse models what  we what we know from the past couple of  years based on single cell rna-seq is  that um of course in in alzheimer's  disease you have the occurrence of these  amyloid plaques  which is a typical phenomenon of the  pathology there but what was discovered  is that you have a specific subset of  microglia that responds to to this  pathology and they're called  disease-associated microglia and you can  identify these subtypes  using single cell rna-seq but i think  it's very important and it's a very nice  use case of the power of spatial biology  to really understand how these  subtypes of microglia  are are responding to the pathology  where they are located and this can  provide a basis for drug discovery and  really modulating the outcome of disease  and just to give you more insight into  this so on the on the left you can see  a comparison of gene expression between  wild type and an alzheimer's disease  mouse model and on the top you see just  the the depiction of the different cell  types neurons  and microglia as well microglia and  green neurons in blue here and on the  bottom you can see that uh when coloring  the the the same cell populations you  can immediately see big differences and  we see  less neurons uh there in the tissue but  also slight increase in in the green  signals meaning the the microglia  but importantly i want to mention here  as well  in yellow you can see here the amyloid  plaques and what was actually done is we  first ran our molecular cartography a  workflow and after that because during  the workflow we have  we  the tissue is completely intact we  maintain the tissue integrity you can  actually after that analysis you use it  still for immunohistochemistry or  labeling it with a dye and in this case  we labeled it with a pftaa dye  which specifically binds to the amyloid  plaques here depicted in yellow  and on the right side this is another  type of analysis we can just visualize  just all the genes that are enriched in  the wild type cortex versus the  alzheimer mouse model cortex and we can  see that  there are major differences of course  and many of these genes are actually  enriched near the amyloid plaques  and again when we segment out  all these cells using dappy with q-pop  again like mentioned before we can we  can we can generate these umap clusters  and really identify all the different  cell populations and just like you would  do with um single cell rna-seq  is you can compare wild-type versus  admos model  clusters specifically and you can  compare  let's say the effect of pathology in in  in in your transcriptomic readout here  and there are many different differences  across many different populations but  what the spatial component allows you to  do and i will go into detail a bit  further  but it allows you to also measure the  distance  towards the plaque center for each  individual  cell in the in this cluster and this is  just say a heat map and we can see that  for microglia for example on so the top  cluster  you can see that  you have two populations one is  related to the wild type the other one  is related to the ad pathology  you can clearly see that it's also  heavily correlated with the distance  towards the plaque so the closer you go  to the plaque the more you see these  cells going into a disease-associated  phenotype  and you can really dive much deeper into  this analysis and  um when we  zoom out a bit again and we just analyze  all of the frequency of all the  different cell types we can clearly see  that when you compare wild type to ad  mouse model brain  you can see a decreasing glutamatergic  neurons and an increase in the number of  microglia but of course  what is important is you have the  spatial component at  at all times what we did here is we  actually correlated that or quantified  actually the uh the distance towards the  closest plaque for all these different  cell types and on the right panel you  can clearly see that  when you go closer to the plaque center  you can see a decrease in the amount of  glutamatergic neurons and you can see an  increase in the number of microglia this  already hints towards  what is happening in response to  pathology  you can even go  deeper into the data and really check  individual transcript levels  so what we actually did is we analyzed  more than 600 individual plaques across  three different  animals and we drew concentric circles  around the plaque and then we measured  for each  transcript how their levels changed in  relation to the distance to the plaque  so and this allows you also on  individual transcript level to really  assess uh potential markers for  different uh cell types so in this case  this is visualized for a marker gene  that is known to be a marker for the  disease associated microglia you can  clearly see in the middle panel that are  located around the amyloid plaques  and on the right panel is a  quantification of this that you can see  for uh two markers so that marker one  and two in green and red you can see an  increase in their expression the closer  you get to the plaque center and at the  same time for some neural markers you  actually see a decrease and this is a  nice readout to have if you're for  example developing a drug and when a  modulated disease you would also like to  see changes in these transcripts but  also on a  cell type level and things like that so  it's i think a very nice application and  shows the power of spatial biology in  the context of new discoveries in  alzheimer's disease  and again so the previous fight was more  on the transcript level but you can all  always zoom out and really look again at  the different cell types and how they  respond to pathology so on the left side  is again  a data set on the ad mouse model and on  the right side you have the wild types  and if you just  map back the different cell types in  this case for example you can nicely see  on the right with the wild types in  purple the astrocytes that are nicely  distributed along the mouse cortex and  the reactive astrocytes that are  on the outer edges of the cortex  you clearly seen the the mouse model  representative for alzheimer's disease  on the left side that  these reactive astrocytes are now  dispersed throughout the cortex and  again these diseases associated  microglia they pop up as a new  population and they surround the uh the  amyloid plaques again labeled with the  pfda  dye  so you can learn a lot about these cell  different cell clusters how they map  back spatially and how they interact  with  with the pathology in this case  and this is  also an aim this is an ongoing work just  to show you that it's possible to also  do this on on human samples so this is  again in collaboration with a a  pharmaceutical company  to really show differences between uh  control human  brain tissue and and post-mortem  alzheimer tissue in black stage stage  six and you can see here just a snapshot  a teaser basically of the different uh  cell populations we're able to visualize  here and you already see clear  differences in in for example the  expression of neural markers here but  this analysis is ongoing and i hope  maybe in the next webinar  dive deeper in in the in the outcome  here and then what you can discover with  uh with our technology on human brain as  well um this shows you that it's uh that  our technology works in different  species on on different sample types etc  and i want to  summarize what is possible with our  technology basically  we are able to apply our technology to  many different species to many different  types of tissues and also different cell  cultures and and cell types  and we can  probe up to 100 genes simultaneously in  each individual's  sample  what i think is very important is that  we offer a very high resolution so it's  single molecule and we are also able to  provide 3d information  across the tissue section  as for analysis which is a very  important part of the whole spatial  technology field at this moment we  actually provide an interactive software  to really allow you to analyze these  images um very intuitively but also it's  compatible with uh open source platforms  like i showed you like surat and and  things like that to really allow for  many different types of analysis  and i think uh very importantly one spot  is one transcript so it really allows  you to quantify our data digitally  so we don't rely on let's say  fluorescent intensity so this is really  a nice way to quantify your  transcriptomic data in a spatial fashion  we also offer the  possibility to do a multi-omic readout  as i mentioned throughout our essay we  make sure that the the tissue remains  intact so you can do  afterwards you can do immunoisochemistry  or or combine it with dyes with with  other types of readouts and a throughput  in the system we are developing in our  workflow  is actually 24 samples run in parallel  for a 100 genes per sample and i think  our sensitivity as i showed before is  very on par with single molecule fish  and this is also part because we don't  use enzymes  and we don't have like an amplification  bias  inserted in our chemistry and this is  one of the differentiators of resolve  biosciences we actually offer the higher  resolution and the high sensitivity and  this is not always the case for example  when you  look at other spatial technologies that  use  rolling circle amplification or when you  have technologies where you need to  extract the rna with permeabilization  onto onto a glass light for for  sequencing and things like that so we  offer both a high sensitivity and a  higher resolution and we maintain our  tissue integrity of course which allows  you to do much of the downstream  analysis  and i want to  end with just an announcement like how  we work today as we offer an end-to-end  service and support so you are able to  access our technology  in service mode so if you have a gene  list available  you can actually order probes with an  online tool we can support you in that  what we actually do is we send our  special resolve slides to you you uh  section your tissue you put the sections  on top of our  glass light you ship the glass light  back and we run the chemistry in-house  and we provide you with all the data you  need  to analyze in and interpret uh interpret  your important biology and we are there  really to uh support you in in  producing the best biology and really to  boost your research  and i want to end with the last slide if  you are interested in our technology  please drop me an email  if you want to make use of our  end-to-end service again don't hesitate  to contact me and if you have any  questions you can also email but of  course we have a q a lined up as well so  feel free to drop a line there so thank  you very much and have a great day
 

